[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Creator in a chef's hat, introducing the video.
AUDIO: 'Here\'s how we color grade real estate videos in DaVinci Resolve. Let\'s cook. All right, let\'s get to it. So, grading real estate footage can be really difficult because you\'re dealing with external, internal, light coming through windows, different color temperature'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'light bulbs, all those kinds of things. It\'s kind of the worst thing to grade, but you also need to do it really quickly because in the real estate world, we\'ve got to get those videos turned around as fast as possible. So, here\'s how we do it. All right, so we\'re'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to start with the easy shot first, the external. So, first of all, you need to get your nodes set up and convert your footage from log to Rec. 709. So, I like to just get nine nodes in there straight away for me to work on. So,'

[00:36] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph and color wheels, with a search bar open for "color space".
AUDIO: 'using [music] option S, I create &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; nine nodes and then organize them into three layers of three. Okay, so'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we\'ve got our nine nodes. The first thing we need to do is transform our log footage into Rec. 709. And the way we do that is with a color space transform effect. So, go to the top right in effects, that\'ll open up your effects'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'window. Search for color space, grab your color space transform, and drag it onto the second last node. Very important, second last node. In here, we need to tell DaVinci what the footage'

[01:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was shot in. Okay, so our input color space is That\'s what we shot the footage in. Our output color space is Rec. 709 and [music] our output gamma is Rec. 709 A.'

[01:25] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve preferences window, specifically the "General" tab with "use Mac display color profiles for viewers" option.
AUDIO: 'Now, we\'re on a Mac. If you\'re on a Windows, you want to set that to gamma 2.4. Also, quick side note, if you\'re on a Mac, go to your top left, go to preferences, in general, make sure you'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have ticked on use Mac display color profiles for viewers. That just make it makes it so what you see on your viewer is what you get after you export. So, we\'ve transformed our log footage into Rec. 709. Now, I\'m going to label all my'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'nodes for organization. So, first of all, the first node is the highlights node. The second is white balance.'

[02:04] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph, with a context menu open for "Node Label".
AUDIO: 'The third is exposure. The fourth, for external shots, I like to play with [music] the sky, so I make a sky node. And then the other nodes between here and the color space transform is up to you. Sometimes I\'ll play with green, so'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'ll have a green node, and then I leave the others blank and I can use them later down the track if I want to. The last node after your color space transform will be for a creative lot, and I\'ll label our color space transform'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as CST. So, the reason why we set up our node graph like that with the color space transform at the end is because nodes are pretty much like layers, so they go left to right, and it brings the'

[02:42] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph with "Color Space Transform" effect applied, and a waveform scope.
AUDIO: 'information from one node into the next, so you\'re losing detail each time. We want to preserve as much detail before we transform it into Rec. 709, [music] cuz as soon as we go into the Rec. 709'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'color space, we lose a lot of colors. Rec. 709 can\'t handle as many colors, so it\'s always good to do any adjustments before the CST. The reason why we have our creative lot after the CST is because most creative lots are made to'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be used on Rec. 709 footage, so it goes after our CST. Okay, first thing we\'re going to do is balance the image, so white balance. It\'s just a good starting point, and I mostly do this off eye, but you can use your waveform down here to'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'help. What we\'re trying to do here is pretty much make our whites white. So, I\'m going to go in the white balance node and go to my temperature and tint controls and adjust it till I feel like it\'s the right colors. [music] So, I feel like it\'s a little blue, I\'m going'

[03:33] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph and waveform scope, with the highlight qualifier active.
AUDIO: 'to push a little bit more warmth in. &gt;&gt; [crying] &gt;&gt; Like that. And tint I\'m not going to touch. Tint Tint can get crazy. Don\'t get crazy with'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tint. So, that looks balanced, looks It\'s Now, the next thing we\'re going to do is go to my highlights node and bring down the highlights because the those really high spots are really bright and I have a really good technique for'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'preserving your highlights without making it look gross. So, in the highlights node, we go to the qualifier tab. We turn off hue and saturation. We turn on the'

[04:11] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the qualifier tab with hue and saturation off, and highlight button on, displaying a monochrome mask of highlights.
AUDIO: 'highlight button. We turn on the highlight button which shows what we\'ve qualified and now I\'m going to drag the low on luminance up until I can only see the'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'really bright parts of the image that I think are too bright. So, let\'s go with that. Next thing to do is add a little bit of low soft which is'

[04:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just adding a bit of feather. And the big one in map finesse, go to denoise and crank that up. And that gives you a really natural roll off of the highlights.'

[04:53] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph and color wheels, with the highlight qualifier turned off and the image color corrected.
AUDIO: 'Great. Now, we\'re going to turn off the highlight button. Go to our primaries and we\'re going to drag down the highlights slider. And you\'ll see those highlights calm'

[05:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'down really naturally. So, I\'m going to set it around there. That looks good. And then if I feel like it\'s missed out some of the brighter spots, I can go back in my qualifier and bring this low down'

[05:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you\'ll see what that does. But I feel like we had it pretty good. So, let\'s leave it right about there. Okay, so our image is balanced and we\'ve preserved the highlights. Exposure, this is pretty well exposed. What I really'

[05:33] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing node graph and color wheels, with the contrast slider being adjusted.
AUDIO: 'like to do is just add a bit of contrast using the contrast slider to give it a bit more punch. And then if I feel like I need to play'

[05:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with the shadows and midtones or the highlights, I use the lift gamma and gain wheels, which are pretty much shadows, midtones, highlights. I might just push a little bit more midtones into this image just to brighten up a'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'little bit. But that looks good to me. Next thing is the sky. With external shots especially, you can get pretty crazy with the sky and you can push it too far. A nice technique you can use is to just [music]'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bring a little more depth to the sky by actually darkening it. And how we do that is we go to the curves tab. We go to the hue versus luminance. We get our little picker. We pick the'

[06:23] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the curves tab, specifically "Hue vs Lum", with a color picker active on the sky.
AUDIO: 'most midtone part of the sky. It\'s going to bring up this and we just bring that down. And that just kind of it\'s technically making the blues less'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bright, but what it looks like is it just kind of richens up the sky a bit. So that looks really nice. The next thing I like to do with external shots is [music] I often play with the greens. This shot doesn\'t have a lot of greens in it, but'

[06:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you have lots of grass, grass can sometimes look really funny. And the way you play around with that is again in the curves tab and you use your hue versus hue and your hue versus sat, your hue versus saturation, to adjust the'

[07:02] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the curves tab, specifically "Hue vs Hue", with a color picker active on green foliage.
AUDIO: 'hues and the saturation of the green. [music] So we\'ve just got these palm trees over here. They look pretty good, but I\'ll show you. We select a bit of the green and often'

[07:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what you\'ll notice is what should be green is actually yellow. So we\'re actually trying to make it more green using the hue versus hue. And I often widen this cuz it\'s'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'too narrow usually when you use the color picker. As you can see, we\'re playing with those greens there. [music]'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now this really sensitive, so you just want to I\'m just going to make it a little more green instead of yellow. There we go. Now, I\'m going to go to the hue versus saturation, try and mimic'

[07:52] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Creator speaking to the camera, happy with the color corrected exterior image.
AUDIO: 'that same section of the curve graph. Make sure all my markers are at one on the saturation. And I\'m just going to saturate the'

[08:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'greens a little more. Just to give it a little more punch. Okay, so I\'ve color corrected the image. Looks really nice. I\'m happy with the balance, the exposure, but it\'s missing the the sauce. So, we\'re going to add a little bit more depth, a bit more'

[08:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'creativity with our LUT. And we\'ve actually developed five of our own LUTs that we use for all of our real estate videos, which you can actually download for free in the description, by the way. So, to add your LUT, you go to your LUTs'

[08:34] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the LUTs panel open on the left, with "RD LUTs" selected and "ambience" highlighted.
AUDIO: 'panel on the top left. In this case, we\'re going to RD LUTs. And my favorite of our LUTs is ambience. It just warms up the image a bit, gives it a bit of depth, doesn\'t go too crazy. So, just right click, apply LUT to'

[08:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'current node. And even then, I like to turn LUTs down a little bit. I find most of them are too much out of the gate. This one\'s pretty good, but just for reference, if you go to the keying node, you can go to key output, and this is'

[08:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pretty much your opacity of this node. So, you can drag it down to take the edge off. But for this LUT, I pretty much keep it at 100% because we made it so good. So, the important thing to remember,'

[09:12] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Creator speaking to the camera, transitioning to interior shots.
AUDIO: 'especially with LUTs, cuz they can go pretty crazy, is we\'re not trying to make a cinematic movie here. We\'re trying to show the [music] house in the best way possible, make it clean, make it realistic. And I personally think adding a bit of warmth'

[09:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is really nice. It helps people connect with the house, I think. So, that\'s why that\'s my favorite LUT. It adds a bit of warmth, bit of glow. It\'s nice. Okay, that was the external shot. Now, we\'re going to go to the'

[09:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'interior, which is slightly more complicated, but more or less the same process. Okay, so this is the interior shot we\'re working with. Classic real estate shot, can be really difficult to grade. We got the outside in the background, really bright. The'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'foreground is the inside, much darker. And we have to try and balance the image as best as we can. So, I started off with the same node graph. I\'ve got the CST in there. I\'m going to go through the same process.'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, I\'m going to balance the image first. In this kind of shot, we have a really bright outside. I first like to tackle the highlights. So, I\'m going to go to my highlight node and do the exact same thing that I did for the externals.'

[10:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Okay, so I selected the really bright part, the outside in this shot. Now, I\'m going to drag down my highlight. And you\'ll see we just bring down that whole exterior part of the shot'

[10:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'down to a better level, so it\'s not completely blown out. Now, some of the information is lost. Like, [music] you\'re just going to have that with real estate. We do our best to bring down those bright parts without making it look unnatural. So, don\'t go too hard on'

[10:40] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the highlight qualifier being used on an interior shot to reduce bright window exposure.
AUDIO: 'this. Now, on the exposure tab, usually with interior shots, there\'s a bit more playing around with the lift, gamma, and gain to balance the image where the dark spots aren\'t too dark and the bright'

[10:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'spots aren\'t too bright without the contrast being weird. So, I\'m going to play around with that now. What I often do with interior shots is I do end up bringing up the shadows a bit. And then I\'ll go back to the highlights'

[11:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to bring down the highlights if I need to just to keep everything in balance. Okay, I think that looks pretty good. The important thing to remember with real estate is you\'ve got to compromise. We\'re just'

[11:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shooting what\'s there. We don\'t bring in any lighting. We\'re dealing with windows. We have no idea what we\'re turning up to when we get there. So, you just have to do the best you can, and the important [music] thing is not to push it too far and make it look'

[11:29] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the curves tab, specifically "Hue vs Sat", with a color picker active on a white wall.
AUDIO: 'unnatural. It\'s better to have blown out windows or really dark parts of the room to avoid a completely unnatural image that just looks terrible, honestly. So, I\'m happy with how this looks.'

[11:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The next huge thing with real estate videos, this is a bit of a trick [music] that a lot of people don\'t know, but what you\'ll often get with these white interiors is the walls will take on this kind of yellow tinge. It\'s'

[11:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'something in the paint and the light and, you know, downlights bouncing off walls and all that. And there\'s a way we can kind of take that yellow tinge out of it. This shot doesn\'t have too bad, but I\'ll show you how it works. We\'re'

[12:08] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve UI showing the curves tab, specifically "Hue vs Sat", with the curve being adjusted to remove yellow tinge.
AUDIO: 'going to jump on another node here that we haven\'t used. We\'re going to label it yellow. We\'re going to go to our curves tab, go to our hue versus saturation, and click on the walls where it\'s a bit'

[12:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got a bit of that yellow tinge. So, on the roof here it looks a little yellow. I\'m going to click there. And you\'ll see actually how red that is. So, I\'m going to widen my curve a little bit. And I\'m just going to pull the'

[12:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'saturation down a little bit just to take out that yellow tinge but without pushing it so much that we take the character and the other brownish, reddish colors out of the image. So, I think that looks good. I haven\'t'

[12:47] [scene:result]
VISUAL: Creator speaking to the camera, discussing the finished interior image.
AUDIO: 'gone too far. Pretty subtle with this one, but with some houses this can make a huge, huge difference. So, last thing I do before I add my light is just make sure that I\'m happy with the image, which I think it looks good. Now, we\'re going to add our light again.'

[13:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'All right, that\'s our finished image. We\'ve just gone for a natural look, a little bit warm, nice and inviting. There is some blown out information in the background, but you\'re just going to get that. If we try to bring that down,'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the interior would be too dark and you would lose all the contrast from the image. So, that\'s how you do an interior. All right, one last big tip is now we have to do this for a bunch of clips. The best way to do that is to save a still for your interior shot and'

[13:27] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DaVinci Resolve gallery tab showing saved stills for exterior and interior shots.
AUDIO: 'your exterior shot. A still is just saving a node tree how it is, so then you can apply it to other clips. So, we\'re in our gallery tab where our stills live. We right click on the viewer,'

[13:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'press grab still, and that\'s our still for the interior. If you want, you can then label it, so you can label it interior. I\'m going to go to my external shot that I graded. I click grab still, and now I\'ve got my'

[13:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exterior and my interior still that I can then use on all of the other clips, and then I can just go through and make adjustments on each clip, [music] and I don\'t have to start from scratch. All right, that\'s how we color grade our real estate videos. Hope that helped,'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and if you want to know anything else about editing, shooting real estate videos, or anything else with videography, comment any questions below. All right. Peace.'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music]'
